// @check
// @hint redundant type annotation: `f` is inferred as `i32`
// @error return type mismatch: expected i32, got string | i32
// THE OTHER HALF of `elseif-chain-residual.vl`'s soundness argument, re-derived after the
// reads-only re-ruling (D1736) took its old premise away.
//
// After `if x is string { return 0 }`, the else arm sees `x` narrowed to `i32`, and the code
// AFTER the chain inherits that residual because the then arm always exits. The old argument
// for trusting the residual was that an arm CANNOT falsify it: a narrowing was also the
// binding's accept contract, so `x = "clobbered"` was a type error inside the arm.
//
// It is not one any more — a write is checked against the DECLARATION and re-narrows the
// binding to what it wrote. So the arm can falsify the residual, and the residual is what
// gives: `checkIfStmtNode` drops it for a name an arm wrote a different member into, and the
// emitter drops the matching push at the same node (`pgResidualVoidOf`). `x` below is back
// at `string | i32` after the chain, which is what the return refuses.
//
// A write the narrowing still admits (`x = 5`) changes nothing: it never retires the
// narrowing, so it is never recorded and the residual stands.
function f(x: string | i32): i32 {
  if x is string { return 0 } else { x = "clobbered" }
  return x
}
print(f(3))
